WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
Welcome to Nestalgia!

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:25.560
And this is the inaugural episode of Nestalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:27.560
Today's game, Ten Yard Fight.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.420
But first we should talk about who we are.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.580
Oh, no, no, no.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.500
You got it. I can't start this.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.900
That's a perfect introduction to Sean.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.680
So I'm Mike Esposito.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:43.240
I'm a producer over at Nickelodeon, but I've loved Nintendo probably since I was about four years old when we got a Nintendo 64.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:48.260
So that outdates me and clearly indicates that we are by no means NES experts.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:55.820
Instead, we are people who are really interested in video games in general and wanted to explore the NES in order of release.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.300
I'm Joe Castu and I'm an aspiring writer.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.320
And Mike was pretty dead on with what I am too as far as my video game experience.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.020
I grew up with my Nintendo, but I'm not an NES expert.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.000
What's up? I'm Sam Smith.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.080
No relation to the artist.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.400
And I'm a PA at Nickelodeon.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.000
I grew up with an N64 for a while before I got anything else.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.440
And so I kind of got a lot of nostalgia with that.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.660
Never played NES before, really before this.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.400
So this is kind of, this is going to be a good time.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.660
And I'm Sean. You do not get to know where I work.

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:32.480
I started everything with the Super Nintendo.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.880
Then I kind of abandoned Nintendo for a while.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.180
I stuck with PlayStation, Xbox, all that fun stuff.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.680
Until recently.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:43.540
So I'm looking forward to playing most of these NES games for the first time.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:49.060
Yeah, so this podcast is really a far cry from a historical analysis of the NES.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:54.480
What we're trying to do here is look at the games in modern view and see if they still hold up.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:59.080
This is something that I think a lot of people wonder when they look at the NES, the Super Nintendo.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:02.320
They play these games and they wonder like, are these games still worth playing?

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:03.960
And I think that's what we're here to answer.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.080
Just kind of getting into the NES launch for a second.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:10.940
The NES launched in 1985, October of 1985.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:16.880
And it comes at a very interesting time because that was pretty much right after the crash of video games in 1983.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.020
And pretty much everybody had abandoned the home console market.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.000
You know, arcades were still somewhat a thing.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.080
The Japanese market remained unaffected.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.720
And computer gaming was still a thing.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.200
So it wasn't like gaming was dead.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:32.440
I think a lot of people think the crash of 83 means no one thought about video games.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:33.460
I don't think that's the truth.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.820
I think what really happened is the home consumer market was just, no, I saw what happened with the Atari 2600.

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:39.860
I don't want to do that again.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.020
Nintendo released the NES in 83 in Japan.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:46.760
And they were seeing good success with it and wanted to move into the American market.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:57.260
And I think what's interesting about this specifically is the jump between what you were seeing in the Atari 2600 and what that console was possible to do.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:05.760
And then the jump all the way up to 8-bit hardware with the NES where you're actually seeing things other than blocks, triangles, like, you know, shapes and figures.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.180
And you were actually able to play, like, full-scale games.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:09.880
But we're not quite there yet.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.980
We're talking about the NES launch games.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:22.480
And that's a little different because these games were mostly arcade ports or games developed directly by Nintendo to complement the release of the system and its features.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.280
Things like Rob, the NES Zapper, Super Mario Brothers.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.820
Even ports of games, their more famous games, like Donkey Kong, didn't come until later.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:42.380
So the NES launch kind of still comes awkwardly to the North American population because the introduction to Nintendo was mostly with Donkey Kong eating all your quarters.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:47.120
And all of these launch games aren't necessarily things that you associate with Nintendo.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.920
Let's jump right into our first game, the game that this episode is all about, Ten Yard Fight.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.740
I don't think it really needs any introduction.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.360
It's a football game that is pretty bare bones, but let's get some initial impressions.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.520
Sean? Well, it's not Madden.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.260
It's definitely football.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:09.060
I enjoyed it from the get-go, to be honest.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:13.660
It was very frustrating, but I'd say my initial impression was positive.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.480
Sam? Yeah, initial impression, it's like, it's cute, you know?

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:23.420
Little guys, they run, they make a noise every time they take a step, which is kind of funny.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:29.180
It did feel, I don't know, it felt like it really had no place to be in a one-player game, I thought,

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:31.160
which I feel like we might talk about in a bit.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.100
But just off the bat, just very kind of, it just felt very basic,

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:40.260
which is kind of what I was expecting going into NES games, but this was just like, just very basic.

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:47.720
Mm-hmm. Joe? So yeah, my initial impression was also positive, but it's not the kind of game that's generally my type of game,

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:49.220
I'm not a big sports game kind of guy.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.220
I'm always a little skewed at the beginning when I start playing a game,

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.120
because I'm always so interested in how they made it work with the limitations.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.880
I mean, I thought it was well done at first.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.200
We'll just say that for now.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.480
And I think my initial impression was, why are they running like that?

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:05.880
Like, what is that formation?

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.260
You know? Oh, the box?

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.120
Yeah, you look at it, and it's like, you know, like, is that the perfect kickoff formation?

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.460
Yeah, that's what football is like.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.340
Yeah, the perfect kickoff formation is the entire team surrounding the receiver.

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:19.980
Just make a giant O, and everybody makes sure the receiver doesn't get touched.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.440
I don't know why anyone doesn't think of that. Tell that to everyone.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.800
Alright, so let's just kind of get into it.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.000
I personally feel like the game is still playable.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.940
It has a lot of flaws in the terms of strategy.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.260
Once you're given the ball, you are immediately sending a man into motion,

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:42.960
which is an interesting little feature, and then that turns into your one receiver.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:48.840
You also have, like, your quarterback is just also your running back and your fullback,

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:52.400
and the three of you line up and run in the same direction.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:57.660
And if you try to, like, walk back a little bit, like, just to get away from people,

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:02.520
all of a sudden you start, you have to, like, kick off the, like, you're punting all of a sudden.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.120
Oh, really? I didn't know it was another thing.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:06.260
You took, like, three steps back.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.800
You know, like, let's say one of the guys was chasing you.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:11.440
You take three steps back, all of a sudden you're punting the ball.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:14.160
It's like, no, I'm just trying to, like, set up a pass.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.480
Wow, now that I didn't actually end up doing.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:20.900
Very interesting. I did that one time, and it wasn't until right now that I realized why that happened.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.560
You just take three steps back and that's it.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.620
But I just want to see if this happened to anybody else.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:30.560
Did anybody else have a computer that threw a very forward-looking pass

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.340
and then proceeded to throw another forward?

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.760
On almost every play.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.040
Okay, so here's the thing.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.920
So this is a 1983 game.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:42.740
When you think about a football game, whether you're watching it or you're playing it.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.940
Oh, real fast. Sean did not misspeak there either.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.500
This is a 1983 game.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.320
It just came to America in 1985.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.980
Yes. Yeah, so this is a 1983 game.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:56.660
When you think of, when you're watching a football game, you think of these lines on the football field

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:04.100
that are just imposed in, like, the director's room or just a box where the director is switching the cameras.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:13.600
That was not a thing until 1998, so you don't actually know where the line of scrimmage is except for, like, it's that specific pixel.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:20.060
So I think when you're thinking of these forward passes that are then immediately thrown to another forward pass,

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:23.460
this is actually a lateral, which you see all the time actually.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:27.900
Right. At the time of this recording, AFC and the NFC Championship games were played.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.940
We saw three flea flickers, which is basically what you're talking about.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:34.400
Okay. Where you flick it to another back and then he throws it again.

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:36.760
This is something that is legal in football.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.980
So I was actually very interested to see that in the game.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:43.960
I am glad for the clarification too because I felt like an idiot when I couldn't then pull this off.

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.820
I was like, okay, wait, so he just did the forward pass twice.

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:49.520
Why can't I? I was passing and then waiting to pass again.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.460
And it just felt like, you know, the computer just had that one little strategy ahead of me.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:56.820
But I might have to go back and try that.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:04.680
Yeah, I think it's really the only way to complete a pass in that game because apparently there is no Z-axis.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:10.900
Yeah, that bugged me the most, I think, in this game was that when you're throwing the ball to your receiver,

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.560
even though it's football and you throw over people to play football, if there's anyone in the way of the ball, they intercept it.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.440
Yeah, even if they're right in front of you, like as if you're throwing like a line drive.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:26.060
Yeah, so you have to make sure that your receiver, so you just basically wait until your receiver is on one side,

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.100
like all the way on one side or all the way on the other and you just pick the side with less defenders.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.160
And that's kind of how you play.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:34.780
I think like when I started off playing the high school team, I'm sure we all did.

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:38.520
And I think I played the first half of that match.

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:45.040
It was just figuring out how to play this game, like so many interceptions, so many, you know, like I just couldn't figure out.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:56.240
I think the second half was even less fun because then I figured out, oh, OK, so just pass it immediately and run to the side as fast, you know, like as diagonally as you can.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.400
Mm-hmm. Gain like five, four, you know, four or five yards.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.640
Do that again, first down and just keep going the whole way.

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:05.160
It's like that's really for me that was the optimal strategy.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:08.580
When I played the high school game, my strategy was just run the ball every play.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:09.900
Oh, so you were running every play.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.080
Every play and it worked better for me than I was passing the ball.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:20.600
I mostly ran the ball. I would usually lateral it to my back so that like there was another like gap that I could run through.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:25.780
But there was also this weird like rubber banding thing that as soon as you got like a certain distance away from the defender.

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:26.980
You just became sluggish.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.820
You were slow and they would just gallop right above.

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:33.200
Yeah, very strange how that would work.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.080
And what about those dive animations?

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.460
I think the dive, I don't know.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:40.320
I feel like me versus the computer, dives suck.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.280
Because like the computer gets the perfect dive and you, you're diving all over the place.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.020
And sometimes you dive like 20 yards.

00:09:46.340 --> 00:10:00.500
Yeah. Good thing there's no Z-axis in this game because if you saw that dive, this person is like literally like they're just jumping like 30 feet in the air and then like flying over you.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:06.760
You also can't, like the game can't keep track of where you are going forward.

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:12.960
So like if you dive sideways and the ball's being run forward, your character diving will also just move forward.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:14.940
Like it stays in the middle of the screen to move forward.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:19.300
Yeah. And pretty much missing a dive in this game is the equivalent of losing like 30.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.740
It's like it will take forever for you to catch them again and get that opportunity.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:28.460
I do think, I didn't play two player with someone else.

00:10:28.580 --> 00:10:29.580
I've just played versus a computer.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:34.340
I think with people it would be a different story because I think the diving would be kind of fun.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:40.620
Because it's kind of like that fun of these controls are really clunky but they're clunky for both of us.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:42.580
So it's like kind of fair, you know what I mean?

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:47.160
Yeah, no, I definitely do understand that and I think Sean you have something to say.

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:58.080
Yeah, I do think that the controls are clunky but it's actually really impressive that they were able, that they were actually able to make all of this stuff possible with those two buttons.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:10:59.460
Yeah, no, I just mean the dive.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:00.900
Yeah, of course, of course.

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.320
But like the whole game feels like an NES game.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:13.200
I think, yeah, I think important here is like what you said that it's impressive that they were able to program an NES game to run a full football pretty much.

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:18.220
But the thing that really kills this game for me is there's no playbook.

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:19.860
There's no stats.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:21.640
There's no challenges.

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:29.560
There's nothing that really, nothing other than the concept of get the ball to your zone, you know, and score the touchdown or the field goal.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:35.960
Nothing, nothing about it otherwise is, is like anything that is reminiscent of football.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:38.900
Like there's so much missing in this game.

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:42.540
I think that it's like this is, this is minimalist football.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:49.800
If you want to describe it as something like you can still create a play by just you waiting until your man in motion is where you want him to be.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:51.740
So is Pong the minimalist tennis?

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:53.380
I mean, yeah, sure.

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:54.660
Okay, no, no, I'm asking.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.300
Yeah, so if Pong, so this is like, this is football.

00:11:57.300 --> 00:11:58.980
Now I'm not saying this is as important.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:05.320
I'm saying this is football's Pong in that sense of like, how do I make a football game that still feels like football?

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:08.160
Yeah. But we don't have, we don't have the ability.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:10.760
Yeah, I think that's a good way of, a really good way of describing it.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:14.960
I think if anything, it's a good blueprint for other football games.

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:18.300
You know, I don't know if there were many football games that came before this one.

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:30.500
Yeah, no, at this point, the football games that you're looking at, besides the ones on the computer, which actually did run some pretty detailed like, you know, not in the sense of not gameplay.

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:34.240
I'm talking about like, you know, kind of the simulation style things.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:35.240
The football games before that.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:39.860
Yeah, we're talking like, we're talking blocks on the Atari 2600 and you know, that kind of stuff.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:41.260
And so this is a, this is a big step.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:45.680
As far as that goes, yeah, I think it is a good blueprint just for games to come.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:49.540
I'll pose this as a question.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:53.180
Like, would something like Madden exist if this never existed?

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:55.260
Would like the form, would it be different, you think?

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:56.800
Or do you think that this was influential?

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:01.320
I don't think that this was influential enough to direct Madden.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:09.760
I think that this game offers a lot in terms of like, here's how, here's how you would play football on a video game console.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:15.120
Yeah. I don't think that it really offers anything in terms of like, I mean, even just consider the gameplay.

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:17.640
I played, I played about six games.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:21.500
And I played from high school all the way up to the Super Bowl team.

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:25.320
And the AI was not that much better in the Super Bowl.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.980
Like, like it didn't feel like any more of a challenge.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.840
That's true. It's so simple.

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:31.540
Right. It's such a simple, yeah, that's what I'm saying.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:36.880
It's such a simple execution that it doesn't feel like there's a lot more to be learned here other than like, you know,

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:40.880
the biggest thing when programming a football game is you got to make sure you can pass and run.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:45.020
Well, that's also, it's also probably because it's coming from, it's an arcade game that got ported.

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:50.500
More so, I think also the, there was only offense on the arcade version.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:52.380
That makes sense. There wasn't defense.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:55.700
Playing defense in that game is frustrating as hell.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:59.220
Yeah. So one of the things, a couple of other things I wanted to talk about.

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:03.380
Real funny. So I did mention that I played the Super Bowl team.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:08.600
When you beat the Super Bowl team, and just for some, some clarification here,

00:14:08.740 --> 00:14:11.880
when you beat the high school team, it says, congratulations, you're on your way to the Super Bowl.

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.100
And so on and so forth.

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:17.240
It says that forever. When you beat the Super Bowl team, I really wanted to know what was going to happen.

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.680
Maybe there would be like some extra animations programmed or something like that.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:23.740
Instead, you get the black screen with the white tech.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:26.020
Congratulations. You're on your way to the Super Bowl.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:27.940
Wow. Next year. I just, yeah.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:31.380
I guess like, yeah, we really are destined to be on the Super Bowl again.

00:14:31.540 --> 00:14:35.100
But I just, I couldn't believe that they were that lazy about it.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:40.020
Yeah. That you would go through the trouble of programming like a whole idea of like, oh, just keep playing

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:40.840
until you get to the Super Bowl.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.160
Not that you can't just pick Super Bowl right from the start.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:46.240
But then to just like, be like, yeah, yeah, just loop back to that screen.

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:47.820
Not even like a, you did it.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:49.620
Like, if you did it, it would have been fine, right?

